     "I agree with you it does look odd.  The syntax you suggest is what I originally tried as seeming natural.  However, LAVAAN gives an error when I use it:<div><br></div><div><div>&gt; tst.Yves.fit=sem(tst.Yves.sem,estimator=&quot;MLR&quot;,verbose=T,data=instosc.df[1:10000,],control=list(eval.max=20000, iter.max=10000),se=&quot;none&quot;,test=&quot;none&quot;)</div><div><br></div><div>Error in getDataFull(data = data, group = group, group.label = group.label,  : </div><div>  lavaan ERROR: missing observed variables in dataset: FA FB FC FD FE FF PC1 PC2</div><div><div><br></div><div>It looks as if LAVAAN is not recognizing  FA FB FC FD FE FF PC1 PC2 as latent variables.  </div><div><br></div><div>I brought this to your attention a couple of weeks ago (direct email) and you said I needed to use =~ operator or LAVAAN wouldn&#39;t know the variables are latent.  OK, but the documentation indicates that  =~ operator is an effect indicator operator which means &quot;is manifested by&quot; .  What I need to communicate to LAVAAN is a causal indicator relationship &quot;is caused by&quot;.  To avoid running the causal arrows in the wrong direction, I tried the syntax we both think looks odd.  It runs without an error statement and gives reasonable results except for the flattening of effects that I mentioned (which may actually be reasonable).  </div><div><br></div><div>Thats what I did, I don&#39;t know if it is right, I don&#39;t know what I am doing, and I am baffled by the syntax for higher order effects in LAVAAN.</div><div><br></div><div>Best MLT</div><div><br></div><div>(test code below)</div><div>&gt; tst.Yves.sem &lt;- &#39;</div><div>+    #Latent variable expression</div><div>+    A1 =~ A1.1 + 1*A1.2 + 1*A1.3 + 1*A1.4</div><div>+ A2 =~ A2.1 + 1*A2.2 + 1*A2.3 + 1*A2.4</div><div>+ A3 =~ A3.1 + 1*A3.2 + 1*A3.3 + 1*A3.4</div><div>+ B1 =~ B1.1 + 1*B1.2 + 1*B1.3 + 1*B1.4</div><div>+ B2 =~ B2.1 + 1*B2.2 + 1*B2.3 + 1*B2.4</div><div>+ B3 =~ B3.1 + 1*B3.2 + 1*B3.3 + 1*B3.4</div><div>+ C1 =~ C1.1 + 1*C1.2 + 1*C1.3 + 1*C1.4</div><div>+ C2 =~ C2.1 + 1*C2.2 + 1*C2.3 + 1*C2.4</div><div>+ C3 =~ C3.1 + 1*C3.2 + 1*C3.3 + 1*C3.4</div><div>+ D1 =~ D1.1 + 1*D1.2 + 1*D1.3 + 1*D1.4</div><div>+ D2 =~ D2.1 + 1*D2.2 + 1*D2.3 + 1*D2.4</div><div>+ D3 =~ D3.1 + 1*D3.2 + 1*D3.3 + 1*D3.4</div><div>+ E1 =~ E1.1 + 1*E1.2 + 1*E1.3 + 1*E1.4</div><div>+ E2 =~ E2.1 + 1*E2.2 + 1*E2.3 + 1*E2.4</div><div>+ E3 =~ E3.1 + 1*E3.2 + 1*E3.3 + 1*E3.4</div><div>+ F1 =~ 1*F1.1 + 1*F1.2 + 1*F1.3 + 1*F1.4</div><div>+ F2 =~ 1*F2.1 + 1*F2.2 + 1*F2.3 + 1*F2.4</div><div>+ F3 =~ 1*F3.1 + 1*F3.2 + 1*F3.3 + 1*F3.4</div><div>+ FA ~ A1+A2+A3 </div><div>+ FB ~ B1+B2+B3 </div><div>+ FC ~ C1+C2+C3 </div><div>+ FD ~ D1+D2+D3 </div><div>+ FE ~ E1+E2+E3 </div><div>+ FF ~ F1+F2+F3 </div><div>+ PC1 ~ FA+FB+FC</div><div>+ PC2 ~ FD+FE+FF</div><div>+ CC ~ PC1+PC2 </div><div>+ CC =~ obCC</div><div>+ # residual variances</div><div>+ A1.1~~v1*A1.1</div><div>+ A1.2~~v1*A1.2</div><div># more lines constraining variances</div></div></div>"
